/target
# Cargo.lock is COMMITTED. This crate is a binary someone installs, not
# a library, so the lockfile is the record of what a release was built
# from. Ignoring it leaves `--locked` nothing to check against.
*.img
.history
.test-env
test-diagnostics/
.claude/.fixtures/

# Test fixtures: real XFS images built by scripts/build-fixtures.sh.
# The *.img rule below already covers what the script writes, but naming
# the directory means a non-image file dropped in here (a build log, a
# manifest) is not swept into a commit by `git add -A`.
.fixtures/
